nes plus 2 sunny lines. Since we can cover all points with n vertical lines, we can try to replace two vertical lines with two sunny lines, but we need to ensure that the sunny lines cover the points that were previously covered by those vertical lines. However, the vertical lines we replace might be columns that have many points, and we need to cover those points with sunny lines. But sunny lines cannot be vertical/horizontal/diagonal, so they cannot be parallel to axes or slope -1. So they cannot be vertical lines. So we cannot replace a vertical line with a sunny line that covers the same column, because any line covering all points in a column would be vertical. So we need to replace vertical lines with sunny lines that cover the points in those columns in a different way, perhaps by using lines that intersect multiple columns.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: can we cover the points of two columns (or some other set) using two sunny lines? For columns n-1 and n, we have three points. As we saw, any line covering two of them is non-sunny. So we cannot cover them with sunny lines. So we need to use non-sunny lines for those columns. So we cannot replace those columns with sunny lines. So we need to replace some other columns.\n\nMaybe we can replace columns 1 and 2 with sunny lines. Column 1 has points (1,1),(1,2),...,(1,n). Column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),...,(2,n-1). Can we cover all points in columns 1 and 2 with two sunny lines? Possibly: we could use a line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1 (sunny). That covers (1,1) and (2,2). But not the rest. We could use a line through (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1, covering (1,2),(2,3). Continue? But we only have two sunny lines, not many. So cannot cover all points in those columns.\n\nThus maybe k=2 is impossible for any n≥3. Let's try to prove impossibility.\n\nWe need to show that any configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines cannot cover all points. Equivalent to: any covering of the triangular grid with n lines must have at